<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Olaf Frederick Nelson</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1883">1883</a>–<a href="/years/1944">1944</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as O. F. Nelson</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Olaf Frederick Nelson was a Samoan merchant of Swedish and Samoan descent and a leader of the Mau, the non-violent movement against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> rule in Western Samoa. The New Zealand administration exiled him twice, in 1928 and 1933. After the police shooting of Mau marchers on Black Saturday in 1929, the movement continued until New Zealand changed course in 1936.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/olaf-frederick-nelson.jpg" alt="Olaf Frederick Nelson" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ta%E2%80%99isi_O.F._Nelson_(cropped).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Nelson was born on 24 February 1883 at Safune on Savai'i, the son of the Swedish trader August Nelson and his Samoan wife, Sina Tugaga, whose family had links to the Sa Tupua, a prominent chiefly family. His name Ta'isi was a matai title from his mother's family at Asau. He grew up in Faleolo, went to the Marist Brothers School in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apia">Apia</a> at eight, and at thirteen became an apprentice at the DH &amp; PG firm, where he founded Samoa's first brass band. He then took over his father's copra business on Savai'i, expanded it across the islands, and by thirty-five was one of the wealthiest members of the Apia community.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>The German administrators had treated Nelson as an equal, but after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> seized control in 1914 he was excluded. Elected to the Legislative Council in 1924, he and the other elected members were constantly overruled by the more numerous government appointees, and he became one of the major forces in the Mau. In May 1927 he founded the newspaper Samoa Guardian. Exiled in January 1928 with two other part-European members of the movement, he took his protests as far as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a> before returning in May 1933.</p><p>The administrator, General Hart, demanded that Nelson be kept out of any fono with the Mau's leaders, and on 15 November 1933 police raided the Mau's headquarters at Vaimoso and Nelson's residence at Tuaefu. Six months after his return Nelson was convicted on three charges connected with the Mau and sentenced to ten more years of exile and eight months' imprisonment in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a>.</p><p>The Full Court of the New Zealand Supreme Court quashed the prison term but upheld the exile, and the Privy Council rejected his appeal. After Labour won the 1935 election the exile was cut short; he returned on 22 July 1936, helped bring about the co-operation agreement between Samoan leaders and the administration, and was elected to the Legislative Council in 1938 and again in 1941.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Nelson died on 28 February 1944; Samoan independence came in 1962. His daughter Malienafau became the first Pacific Island graduate of the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/auckland">Auckland</a>, taking a law degree in 1936, and his daughter Noue married Tupua Tamasese Mea'ole, who became Joint Head of State at independence. Their son became Tui Atua Tupua Tamasese Tupuola Ta'isi Efi, the third Prime Minister of Samoa and later its Head of State. The Nelson family gave the Nelson Memorial Public Library to the Samoan people in his memory.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Olaf Frederick Nelson” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Olaf Frederick Nelson</b>, one of the leaders, was frustrated by the exclusion of native and part-Samoans from governance, and in 1926 he went to Wellington to lobby the New Zealand government for increased self-rule.</blockquote><a href="/movement/mau-movement">Mau movement · Aims</a></li><li><blockquote>Resentment fed the Mau movement of non-cooperation, led by the merchant <b>Olaf Frederick Nelson</b> and Tupua Tamasese Lealofi III.</blockquote><a href="/state/territory-of-western-samoa">Territory of Western Samoa</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apia">Apia</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/samoa">Samoa</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-13.83,-171.83&amp;year=1922" aria-label="Apia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="0 2679 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Olaf Frederick Nelson"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M293 2949h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M293 2949h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=-13.83,-171.83&amp;year=1922">What was here in 1922? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
